MIL-101 $(\mathrm{Cr})$ is built up from a hybrid supertetrahedral building unit formed by terephthalate ligands and trimeric chromium octahedral clusters and offers high surface area, large pore windows $12\mathrm{~\AA~}$ and $16\mathrm{~\AA~}\times\mathrm{~14}.5\mathrm{~\AA~}$ , mesoporous pores $(29-34{\mathring{\mathrm{A}}})$ , and excellent chemical and solvent stability.3639 All of the above-mentioned properties make MIL-101 $(\mathrm{Cr})$ attractive for HPLC separation. Here we report the slurry-packed $\mathrm{MIL-}101\mathrm{(Cr)}$ column for HPLC separation of xylene, dichlorobenzene and chlorotoluene isomers, and EB and styrene because of their important roles in the chemical industry.4044 C8 alkylaromatics such as xylenes, EB, and styrene are the most important raw chemicals in industry.40,41 For instance, $p$ -xylene is oxidized to terephthalic acid for poly(ethylene terephthalate) production, and $m$ -xylene is mostly used in the manufacture of isophthalic acid, while $o$ -xylene is largely used to produce phthalic anhydride. EB is dehydrogenated to styrene for polystyrene production. Their separations have received great attention in spite of technical challenges resulting from their coherent boiling points (EB, $136.2\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C};$ $p$ -xylene, $138.4~^{\circ}\mathrm{C};$ $m$ -xylene, ${139}~^{\circ}\mathrm{C};$ $o$ -xylene, $144^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ ) and dimensions $(0.58-0.68~\mathrm{nm}$ ).42,43 Other isomers such as dichlorobenzene and chlorotoluene are also widely used as solvents or intermediates in the production of insecticides or other pesticides.44
In this work, the MIL-101 $\langle\mathrm{Cr}\rangle$ column was explored as a novel stationary phase for HPLC separation of xylene, dichlorobenzene, chlorotoluene isomers, EB, and styrene. The slurrypacked MIL-101 $\mathrm{(cr)}$ column gave high resolution for the separation of the xylene, dichlorobenzene, and chlorotoluene isomers with high column efficiency and preferable affinity to the ortho-isomers. Fast and selective separation of ortho-isomers from the other isomers was achieved on the MIL-101 $\left(\mathrm{Cr}\right)$ packed column within $3~\mathrm{min}$ . The effects of the mobile phase composition, injected sample mass, and temperature were also investigated.
## EXPERIMENTAL SECTION
Chemicals and Reagents. All chemicals and reagents used were at least of analytical grade. Ultrapure water $\mathrm{^{\prime}18.\bar{2}M\Omega c m},$ was obtained from a WaterPro water purification system (Labconco Corp., Kansas City, MO). $\mathrm{Cr}\big(\mathrm{NO}_{3}\big)_{3}{\cdot}9\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ (Wenda Rare Reagent Co. Ltd., Tianjin, China) and terephthalic acid and hydrofluoric acid $(40.0\%)$ (Aladdin Chemistry Co. Ltd., Shanghai, China) were used to prepare MIL-101 $\left(\mathrm{Cr}\right)$ . Xylene, dichlorobenzene, and chlorotoluene isomers (Aladdin Chemistry Co. Ltd.), EB and toluene (Guangfu Fine Chemical Research Institute, Tianjin, China), styrene (Tianjin Chemical Reagent No.1 Plant, Tianjin, China) were used as the analytes. Hexane, dichloromethane (DCM), $N,N.$ -dimethylformamide (DMF), and ethanol (Guangfu Fine Chemical Research Institute) were used as the solvents. Caution: Care should be taken to avoid direct contact with substituted aromatics and hydrofluoric acid because of their high toxicity and to prepare all the solutions in a well-ventilated hood!
Synthesis of MIL-101(Cr). MIL-101(Cr) was synthesized according to Ferey et al.36 Typically, $\mathrm{Cr}\big(\mathrm{NO}_{3}\big)_{3}{\cdot}9\mathrm{H}_{2}\dot{\mathrm{O}}$ $\mathrm{800mg,}$ $2.0\ \mathrm{mmol}\cdot$ ), terephthalic acid $\left(332~\mathrm{mg},2.0~\mathrm{mmol}\right)$ , and HF $\left(0.1\mathrm{mL},0.5\mathrm{mmol}\right),$ were mixed with ultrapure water $(9.6\mathrm{mL})$ in a Teflon-lined bomb. The Teflon-lined bomb was then sealed and placed in an oven at $220^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{8\mathrm{~h~}}$ . After being cooled to room temperature, the resultant green crystalline solid was washed thoroughly with DMF and ethanol and collected by centrifugation at $10000~\mathrm{rpm}$ for 5 min. The procedure was repeated at least three times to remove the unreacted terephthalic acid from MIL-101 $\left(\mathrm{Cr}\right)$ pores. The green solid was then evacuated in vacuum at $150^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{12\mathrm{h}}$ to form dehydrated MIL-101(Cr), which was characterized by X-ray diffraction (XRD) spectrometry, thermal gravimetric analysis (TGA), and scanning electron microscopy (SEM).
Instrumentation. The XRD patterns were recorded with a $\mathrm{D}/\mathrm{max}{-2500}$ diffractometer (Rigaku, Japan) using $\mathrm{CuK}_{\mathrm{a}}$ radiation $\acute{\lambda}=1.5418\:\mathring{\mathrm{A}}_{\cdot}$ ). The TGA experiments were performed on a PTC-10A thermal gravimetric analyzer (Rigaku, Japan) from room temperature to $800^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ at a ramp rate of $10\mathrm{^{\circ}C\bar{m i n}^{-1}}$ . The SEM images were recorded on a Shimadzu SS-550 scanning electron microscope at $15.0\mathrm{kV}$ .
The HPLC system consisted of a Waters 510 HPLC pump and a 486 tunable absorbance detector. Data acquisition and processing were performed on an N2000 chromatography data system. The Ameritech CO-5060 column heater was used to control the column temperature during HPLC separation.
Preparation of the MIL-101(Cr) Packed Column for HPLC. A $\ensuremath{1.30\mathrm{~g~}}$ mass of MIL-101 $\left(\mathrm{Cr}\right)$ was dispersed in a vessel with $50~\mathrm{mL}$ of DCM under ultrasonication for $\ensuremath{5}\mathrm{{min}}$ . The suspension was then packed into a stainless steel column $(5\ \mathrm{cm\long\x}$ $4.6\mathrm{mm}$ i.d.) under 6000 psi for $10\mathrm{min}$ . The MIL-101 $\langle\mathrm{Cr}\rangle$ packed column was conditioned with DCM at a flow rate of $\mathrm{i}\mathrm{mL}\mathrm{min}^{-1}$ for $^{2\mathrm{h}}$ before chromatographic experiments. Hexane and DCM were used to prepare the mobile phase.
Calculation of Thermodynamic Parameters. The enthalpy change $(\Delta H)$ and entropy change $(\Delta S)$ for the transfer of solutes from the mobile phase to the stationary phase MIL-101 $\langle\mathrm{Cr}\rangle$ were calculated from the following van’t Hoff equation:29
$$
\ln{k^{\prime}}=\mathrm{~-~}\frac{\Delta H}{R T}+\frac{\Delta S}{R}+\ln{\Phi}
$$
where $k^{\prime}$ is retention factor, $R$ the gas constant, $T$ the absolute temperature, and $\Phi$ the phase ratio. $k^{\prime}$ was calculated according to the following equation:
$$
k^{\prime}=(t{-}t_{0})/t_{0}
$$
where $t$ is the retention time for the analyte and $t_{0}$ is the column void time, which was determined by injecting a small plug of hexane and recording the perturbation signal.
## RESULTS AND DISCUSSION
Characterization of the Synthesized MIL-101(Cr). The experimental XRD pattern of the synthesized MIL-101(Cr) crystals was in good agreement with the simulated one, showing the successful preparation of MIL-101 $\left(\mathrm{Cr}\right)$ (Figure 1A).
The TGA data revealed that the MIL-101(Cr) was stable up to $330~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ (Figure 1B). The SEM image exhibited cubic shaped crystals of MIL-101( $\left(\mathrm{Cr}\right)$ with an average size of $1\mu\mathrm m$ (Figure 1C).
HPLC Separation of EB and Xylene Isomers on MIL-101- (Cr). The separation of EB and xylene isomers is challenging because of their coherent boiling points and dimensions.2528 Here we first tested the MIL-101 $(\mathrm{Cr})$ packed column for the separation of EB and xylene isomers using hexane/DCM (95:5) as the mobile phase at a flow rate of $1\mathrm{mL}\mathrm{min}^{-1}$ (Figure 2A). All four isomers in the mixture of xylene isomers and EB were baseline separated on the MIL-101 $\left(\mathrm{Cr}\right)$ packed column. In addition, the $\mathrm{\bar{MIL}{-}}101(\mathrm{Cr})$ packed column offered high column efficiency (20000 plates $\mathrm{m}^{-1}$ for EB) (Figure 2A) and good precision for five replicate separations of xylene isomers and EB $(0.3\mathrm{-}0.4\%$ , $1.5\mathrm{-}2.9\%$ , $0.5\mathrm{-}2.1\%,$ and $1.5\mathrm{-}2.7\%$ (relative standard deviation, RSD) for the retention time, peak area, peak height, and half peak width, respectively) (Figure 2A and Table 1).
The MIL-101 $(\mathrm{Cr})$ packed column showed high selectivity for the ortho-substituted isomers (Figure 2). All the ortho-substituted isomers exhibited stronger retention than the meta- and para-substituted isomers on the MIL-101 $\mathrm{(cr)}$ packed column. The high affinity of MIL-101 $(\mathrm{Cr})$ for $o$ -xylene likely resulted from the interaction between the two methyl groups in $\textit{o}$ -xylene and the terephthalate pore walls of MIL-101 $(\mathrm{Cr})$ . The preferable retention for $\textit{o}$ -xylene was also observed on MIL-53(Al) with the same terephthalate pore walls but different topology structures.27 In addition, the highest polarity of $o$ -xylene (dipole moment $0.54\mathrm{~D~}_{.}^{\cdot}$ ) among the EB and xylene isomers also resulted in the strongest retention of $o$ -xylene on the MIL-101 $\left(\mathrm{Cr}\right)$ packed column.45
HPLC Separation of Dichlorobenzene and Chlorotoluene Isomers on MIL-101(Cr). The MIL-101(Cr) packed column also gave good separation of dichlorobenzene (Figure 2B) and chlorotoluene (Figure 2C) isomers using hexane/DCM (90:10) and hexane/DCM (95:5), respectively, as the mobile phase at a flow rate of $0.5~\mathrm{{mL}~\mathrm{{min}^{-1}}}$ with high column efficiency (13000 plates $\mathrm{m}^{-1}$ for $m$ -dichlorobenzene, Figure 2B; 10000 plates $\mathrm{m}^{-1}$ for $m$ -chlorotoluene, Figure 2C) and good precision $(0.2\mathrm{-}0.3\%$ , $1.1-2.9\%$ , $0.7{-}2.0\%,$ , and $1.4\mathrm{-}2.2\%$ (RSD, $n=5$ ) for the retention time, peak area, peak height and half peak width, respectively) (Table 1).
The MIL-101 $\langle\mathrm{Cr}\rangle$ packed column also gave preferable retention of the ortho-isomers for chlorine-substituted benzene isomers (e.g., dichlorobenzene and chlorotoluene isomers). The elution sequence of dichlorobenzene and chlorotoluene isomers followed an increasing order of meta-isomer $\angle$ para-isomer $\angle$ ortho-isomer. Higher affinity for $\textit{o}$ -dichlorobenzene and $o$ -chlorotoluene on MIL-101(Cr) resulted from the higher polarity and larger dimension of ortho-isomers than the meta- and paraisomers.46
MIL-101(Cr) gave exceptionally high selectivity for orthoisomers even using a more polar solvent such as pure DCM as the mobile phase (Figure 3). In spite of the poor resolution for the meta- and para-isomers, selective separation of ortho-isomers was still achieved within $3\mathrm{min}$ . The above results suggested that the MIL-101 $\mathrm{\langleCr\rangle}$ packed column allowed a fast and selective separation or removal of ortho-isomers from the other substituted position isomers.
HPLC Separation of EB and Styrene on MIL-101(Cr). The separation of EB and styrene, another two important raw materials with very similar dimensions in the chemical industry, was also examined on the MIL-101 $\left(\mathrm{Cr}\right)$ packed column using hexane as the mobile phase at a flow rate of $0.5\mathrm{\mL\min^{-1}}$ (Figure 2D). High-performance separation of EB and styrene was achieved on the MIL-101 $\left(\mathrm{Cr}\right)$ packed column with a resolution of 3.4. High affinity for styrene on the MIL-101 $\left(\mathrm{Cr}\right)$ packed column likely resulted from the $\pi{-}\pi$ interaction between the styrene and aromatic framework walls of MIL-101 $\left(\mathrm{Cr}\right)$ and the coordinative interaction of styrene with the unsaturated metal sites in MIL-101 $\left(\mathrm{Cr}\right)$ . The same elution sequence of EB and styrene was also observed on HKUST-1 with Cu unsaturated metal sites.30,37 Furthermore, a mixture of EB and styrene with their typical impurities of toluene and $o$ -xylene were also well separated on the MIL-101 $\left(\mathrm{Cr}\right)$ packed column (Figure 1D) with good precision $(0.2\mathrm{-}0.4\%,0.9\mathrm{-}1.5\%,0.6\mathrm{-}1.7\%,$ and $0.6\mathrm{-}1.7\%$ (RSD, $n=5$ ) for the retention time, peak area, peak height, and half peak width, respectively) (Table 1).
Effect of the Composition of the Mobile Phase. Almost all the previous studies on the utilization of MOFs for HPLC separation have been performed exclusively with a single pure solvent, either low polar or nonpolar, as the mobile phase, such as heptane or hexane,2531 even though the composition of the mobile phase can play significant roles in retention, selectivity, and resolution in HPLC. Here we take dichlorobenzene isomers as an example to investigate the effect of the mobile phase on HPLC separation on the MIL-101(Cr) packed column. The results showed that the mobile phase composition significantly affected both the retention and the resolution of dichlorobenzene isomers on the MIL-101 $\langle\mathrm{Cr}\rangle$ packed column (Figure 4). The use of pure hexane as the mobile phase gave a poor resolution and long retention time for the separation of $p$ - dichlorobenzene and $o$ -dichlorobenzene. As the content of DCM in the binary hexane/DCM mobile phase increased, the retention time of the analytes decreased accompanied by improved resolution and separation efficiency, most probably as a consequence of an increasing competition from solvent adsorption via van der Waals forces.28 Baseline separation of the dichlorobenzene isomers was achieved on the MIL-101(Cr) packed column with hexane/DCM (90:10) as the mobile phase. The above results revealed that the mobile phase played a significant role in the high-performance separation of dichlorobenzene isomers on the MIL-101(Cr) packed column.
Effect of the Analyte Mass. An increase in analyte mass resulted in a linear increase of the chromatographic peak area and peak height, but almost no change in the retention time (Figure 5). These features of the MIL-101 $\left(\mathrm{Cr}\right)$ packed columns are favorable for its application in separation and preparation of EB and xylene isomers.
Effects of Temperature. To gain insight into the retention of xylene, dichlorobenzene, chlorotoluene isomers, EB, and styrene on the MIL-101 $\left(\mathrm{Cr}\right)$ packed column, their mixtures were separated at temperatures from 20 to $35^{\circ}\mathrm{C}.$ . The retention time of the analytes on the MIL-101 $\langle\mathrm{Cr}\rangle$ packed column was substantially reduced as the column temperature increased (Figure 6), indicating the separation processes of the analytes on $\mathrm{ML}{\cdot}101\mathrm{(Cr)}$ were exothermic. The selectivity for meta- and para-isomers, EB, and styrene slightly decreased as the temperature increased, while the selectivity for ortho-isomers even slightly increased at high temperature (Table 2). The results also confirmed the high selectivity of MIL-101 $\mathrm{(Cr)}$ for ortho-isomers.
The van’t Hoff plots for the separation of xylene, dichlorobenzene, and chlorotoluene isomers, EB, and styrene showed good linearity (Figure 7), suggesting no changes in the interaction mechanism in relation to temperature in the studied temperature range. The $\Delta H$ term represents the energy change involved when the solute molecules transfer from the mobile phase to the stationary phase. When the solute interacts with the stationary phase, the solute molecules are held more tightly and, consequently, are more restricted. This motion restriction, reduced freedom of movement, or loss of randomness is measured as $\Delta s$ .47 The values of $\Delta H$ and ΔS obtained from the van’t Hoff plots are summarized in Table 3. Various contributions of enthalpy and entropy effects depending on the solute constitution were observed on the MIL-101(Cr) packed column. In principle, more positive (or less negative) $\Delta{s}$ and more negative $\Delta H$ should be thermodynamically favorable for the transfer of the solute from the mobile phase to the stationary phase, thus stronger retention for the solute on the stationary phase. The comparable values of $\Delta H$ and the least negative values of $\Delta S$ for ortho-isomers in comparison with the two other corresponding isomers indicate the high selective separation of ortho-isomers on MIL-101(Cr) was controlled by ΔS. The comparable ΔS value of EB and styrene suggested their separation was not based on ΔS, but controlled by $\Delta H$ .
## ’CONCLUSIONS
In summary, we have demonstrated $\mathrm{MIL-}101\mathrm{(Cr)}$ as a promising stationary phase for HPLC separation of substituted aromatics. The $\mathrm{MIL-}101\mathrm{(Cr)}$ packed column gave baseline separation of EB and xylene isomers, dichlorobenzene and chlorotoluene isomers, and EB and styrene with high column efficiency and good precision. The specific high affinity of $\mathrm{{MIL-}101(C r)}$ for orthoisomers allowed fast and selective separation of ortho-isomers from the other isomers. The permanent nanoscale porosity, adsorption affinity, high surface area, and open metal sites make MOFs advantageous for applications in HPLC. Further research should pay more attention to the potential of MOFs for the specific recognition and separation of large molecules, even for large biological molecules.
